 ## Unreleased
 
+- Flash Next: a shared pass gathers every stream's n-gram rows in one pool call (TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_EARLY=1, at most 64 lanes), can lock the tables' scale arrays in memory at load (TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_LOCK_SCALES=1, NVFP4), and TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_LOG=1 prints what the pass launched ahead and waited; every gathered row is the per-stream call's (#9999).
 - Flash Next on CUDA: a prompt pass fills its chunks without draining the stream between them on one rank (`TF_FLASHNEXT_PROMPT_SYNC=1` restores the drain; ranks above one keep it), takes 3,072-row chunks by default, and points MXFP8 projections of 1,024 columns or more at a relaxed 128 x 128 prompt tile (`TF_FLASHNEXT_PROMPT_MX128`, 0 keeps the exact kernels) whose UE8M0 block scales ride in the mma B fragment over one fp32 chain per K; a row's sums keep their order whatever the chunk holds, but they are not the exact kernels' bits (#9999).
 - Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on CUDA: `--kv-cache fp8` keeps keys and values as e4m3 codes with a power-of-two scale a row (#9999). 1,056 bytes a position and layer instead of 2,048, so a token's caches take
   about 17.5 KiB instead of 30 KiB and a Spark's sequence memory admits five full-window streams where bf16 admits three;
